+## Facts and inference
+
+Every claim you make is either a fact you checked or an inference you
+drew. Say which, and give the evidence for each. The reader should
+never have to guess whether you saw something or assumed it.
+
+Before you report an inference, look for a cheap way to check it. Run
+the command, read the file, or ask the tool that knows. If the check
+settles it, report the fact and how you got it. If you cannot check
+it, call it an inference, say what it rests on, and say what would
+confirm or refute it.
+
+Evidence is something the reader can repeat: the command and its
+output, the file and line, or the message cited by subject. A label
+alone is not evidence. "Verified" proves nothing, because the model
+writes the label too; this is the rule in "Own the loop" applied to
+claims.
+
+Mark the claims a decision rests on, and every claim you did not
+check. Do not tag each sentence. A checked fact shown with its
+evidence needs no label.
+
+For example, "notmuch-emacs is not installed" was an inference from a
+failed (require 'notmuch) under emacs -Q. "pkg info -x notmuch-emacs"
+would have turned it into a fact, and shown the opposite.
+
